{"id": "M5-c001", "lesson": "L01", "front": "What is an upper bound of a set A of real numbers?", "back": "A real number u with a ≤ u for every a in A. A set with an upper bound is bounded above (Section 1)."}
{"id": "M5-c002", "lesson": "L01", "front": "What is the supremum of A?", "back": "An upper bound s of A with s ≤ u for every upper bound u of A, that is, a least upper bound. Two suprema of one set are equal (Section 1)."}
{"id": "M5-c003", "lesson": "L01", "front": "State the completeness of the real numbers (Definition 1.1).", "back": "Every nonempty subset of R that is bounded above has a supremum in R. It is the defining property of R, taken without proof."}
{"id": "M5-c004", "lesson": "L01", "front": "Theorem 1.2 (Archimedean property). Hypotheses: x is a real number; every nonempty subset of R bounded above has a supremum; N is the set of natural numbers, and n in N implies n + 1 in N. What is the conclusion?", "back": "There is n in N with n > x.", "grade": "T", "basis": "Proof in Section 2 (Theorem 1.2)"}
{"id": "M5-c005", "lesson": "L01", "front": "Theorem 1.3 (nested intervals). Hypotheses: for each n in N, a_n ≤ b_n are real and I_n = [a_n, b_n]; I_{n+1} ⊆ I_n for every n; every nonempty subset of R bounded above has a supremum. What is the conclusion?", "back": "There is a real s with s in I_n for every n.", "grade": "T", "basis": "Proof in Section 2 (Theorem 1.3)"}
{"id": "M5-c006", "lesson": "L01", "front": "Corollary 1.4 (a single point). Hypotheses: for each n in N, a_n ≤ b_n are real and I_n = [a_n, b_n]; I_{n+1} ⊆ I_n for every n; every nonempty subset of R bounded above has a supremum; and the lengths b_n − a_n tend to 0. What does the intersection of the I_n contain?", "back": "Exactly one real number.", "grade": "T", "basis": "Proof in Section 2 (Corollary 1.4)"}
{"id": "M5-c007", "lesson": "L01", "front": "Lemma 1.5. For S = {x ≥ 0 : x² < 2} and a real m ≥ 0, what does the two-case test say?", "back": "Either m is in S, when m² < 2, or m is an upper bound of S, when m² ≥ 2. In particular 2 is an upper bound of S.", "grade": "T", "basis": "Proof in Section 3 (Lemma 1.5)"}
{"id": "M5-c008", "lesson": "L01", "front": "Theorem 1.6 (bisection brackets the supremum). Hypotheses: S0 is a nonempty subset of R bounded above; a_0 is in S0, b_0 is an upper bound of S0, and a_0 ≤ b_0; for every m with a_0 ≤ m ≤ b_0, either m is in S0 or m is an upper bound of S0. What does bisection give?", "back": "For every n, a_n ≤ sup S0 ≤ b_n, and b_n − a_n = (b_0 − a_0)/2^n. The midpoint replaces a_n when it lies in S0, and replaces b_n otherwise.", "grade": "T", "basis": "Proof in Section 3 (Theorem 1.6)"}
{"id": "M5-c009", "lesson": "L01", "front": "Proposition 1.7. Hypotheses: S = {x ≥ 0 : x² < 2}, and s = sup S, which exists because 0 is in S and 2 is an upper bound (Lemma 1.5). What is s²?", "back": "s² = 2. Both s² < 2 and s² > 2 are impossible.", "grade": "T", "basis": "Proof in Section 3 (Proposition 1.7)"}
{"id": "M5-c010", "lesson": "L01", "front": "In Lab 1, what bracket does bisection give after 12 steps from [0, 2], and what is its width?", "back": "[181/128, 2897/2048], with width 1/2048. The brackets are exact fractions.", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1 (exact rational brackets over 12 steps)"}
{"id": "M5-c011", "lesson": "L01", "front": "In Lab 1, which step is the first whose bracket width is below 1/1000?", "back": "Step 11, where the width is 1/1024.", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1 (exact width comparison; Problem E2 gives the same step from 2^(1−n))"}
{"id": "M5-c012", "lesson": "L01", "front": "In Lab 1, how many of the 13 brackets satisfy a² < 2 ≤ b²?", "back": "All 13.", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1 (exact check over the finite run)"}
{"id": "M5-c013", "lesson": "L01", "front": "Does the set of rational x ≥ 0 with x² < 2 have a least upper bound among the rational numbers?", "back": "No. A rational least upper bound q would satisfy q² = 2, and no rational number does (M1, Lesson 2, Corollary 2.6).", "grade": "T", "basis": "Section 4 (canon break 1), from Proposition 1.7 and M1, Lesson 2, Corollary 2.6"}
{"id": "M5-c014", "lesson": "L01", "front": "Does a bounded set always have a largest element?", "back": "No. S = {x ≥ 0 : x² < 2} is bounded above by 2, but its supremum s satisfies s² = 2, so s is not in S and S has no largest element (Misconception 1)."}
{"id": "M5-c015", "lesson": "L01", "front": "Why does Theorem 1.6 apply to S = {x ≥ 0 : x² < 2}, even though the rational set S_rat has no least upper bound among the rationals?", "back": "S is a nonempty subset of R bounded above by 2 (Lemma 1.5), so Definition 1.1 supplies its supremum in R. Completeness is a property of R, not of the rational numbers (Misconception 2)."}
{"id": "M5-c016", "lesson": "L01", "front": "Does bisection with enough steps land exactly on the supremum?", "back": "No. Every bracket endpoint is rational, and a rational supremum would have square 2, which is impossible (Misconception 3)."}
{"id": "M5-c017", "lesson": "L02", "front": "When does a sequence (s_n) of real numbers converge to L (Definition 1.1)?", "back": "When for every ε > 0 there is N in N with |s_n − L| < ε for every n ≥ N."}
{"id": "M5-c018", "lesson": "L02", "front": "Proposition 1.2 (uniqueness of the limit). Hypotheses: s_n → L and s_n → L' for real numbers L and L'. What is the conclusion?", "back": "L = L'. So the notation lim s_n is meaningful when the limit exists.", "grade": "T", "basis": "Proof in Section 1 (Proposition 1.2)"}
{"id": "M5-c019", "lesson": "L02", "front": "What is a Cauchy sequence (Definition 2.1)?", "back": "A sequence for which every ε > 0 has an N with |s_m − s_n| < ε for all m, n ≥ N. The N depends on ε, and no limit appears in the definition."}
{"id": "M5-c020", "lesson": "L02", "front": "Proposition 2.2 (convergent implies Cauchy). Hypothesis: s_n → L for some real L. What is the conclusion?", "back": "The sequence (s_n) is Cauchy.", "grade": "T", "basis": "Proof in Section 2 (Proposition 2.2)"}
{"id": "M5-c021", "lesson": "L02", "front": "Lemma 2.3. Hypothesis: (s_n) is a Cauchy sequence of real numbers. What is the conclusion?", "back": "There is a real C with |s_n| ≤ C for every n. A Cauchy sequence is bounded.", "grade": "T", "basis": "Proof in Section 2 (Lemma 2.3)"}
{"id": "M5-c022", "lesson": "L02", "front": "Lemma 2.4 (greatest lower bound). Hypotheses: B is a nonempty set of reals bounded below; every nonempty subset of R bounded above has a supremum. What is the conclusion?", "back": "B has a greatest lower bound L, and for every η > 0 some b in B satisfies b < L + η.", "grade": "T", "basis": "Proof in Section 2 (Lemma 2.4)"}
{"id": "M5-c023", "lesson": "L02", "front": "Theorem 2.5 (Cauchy criterion for real sequences). Hypotheses: (s_n) is a Cauchy sequence of real numbers; every nonempty subset of R bounded above has a supremum. What is the conclusion?", "back": "(s_n) converges to some L in R.", "grade": "T", "basis": "Proof in Section 2 (Theorem 2.5)"}
{"id": "M5-c024", "lesson": "L02", "front": "What does it mean for the series Σ a_n to converge (Definition 3.1)?", "back": "The partial sums S_N, each the sum of a_k for k from 1 to N, converge, and the sum of the series is their limit."}
{"id": "M5-c025", "lesson": "L02", "front": "Corollary 3.2 (Cauchy criterion for series). Hypotheses: (a_n) is a sequence of real numbers with partial sums S_N; every nonempty subset of R bounded above has a supremum. What is the conclusion?", "back": "Σ a_n converges if and only if for every ε > 0 there is N with |the sum of a_k for k from n+1 to m| < ε for all m > n ≥ N.", "grade": "T", "basis": "Proof in Section 3 (Corollary 3.2)"}
{"id": "M5-c026", "lesson": "L02", "front": "Proposition 3.3 (harmonic partial sums are not Cauchy). Hypothesis: H_n = the sum of 1/k for k from 1 to n, for integers n ≥ 1. What is the conclusion?", "back": "H_{2N} − H_N ≥ 1/2 for every integer N ≥ 1, so the harmonic series diverges.", "grade": "T", "basis": "Proof in Section 3 (Proposition 3.3)"}
{"id": "M5-c027", "lesson": "L02", "front": "Lemma 3.4 (a geometric tail). Hypotheses: G_N = the sum of 1/2^k for k from 1 to N, with G_0 = 0; n and m are integers with 0 ≤ n < m. What is G_m − G_n?", "back": "1/2^n − 1/2^m, so 0 < G_m − G_n < 1/2^n.", "grade": "T", "basis": "Proof in Section 3 (Lemma 3.4)"}
{"id": "M5-c028", "lesson": "L02", "front": "Proposition 3.5. Hypotheses: G_N as in Lemma 3.4, ε = 1/2^10, and N an integer with N ≥ 0. What is the conclusion?", "back": "|G_m − G_n| < ε for all m > n ≥ N if and only if N ≥ 10.", "grade": "T", "basis": "Proof in Section 3 (Proposition 3.5)"}
{"id": "M5-c029", "lesson": "L02", "front": "If the terms of a series tend to zero, does the series converge?", "back": "No. The terms 1/n tend to zero, but the harmonic series diverges (Proposition 3.3). Tending to zero is necessary for convergence and not sufficient."}
{"id": "M5-c030", "lesson": "L02", "front": "If consecutive terms get closer together, is the sequence Cauchy?", "back": "Not necessarily. For s_n = √n the gaps tend to zero, but the sequence is unbounded, and a Cauchy sequence is bounded (Lemma 2.3)."}
{"id": "M5-c031", "lesson": "L02", "front": "Does a Cauchy sequence of rational numbers have a rational limit?", "back": "Not necessarily. The left bisection endpoints of Lesson 1 form a Cauchy sequence of rationals whose limit sup S is not rational (Section 4)."}
{"id": "M5-c032", "lesson": "L02", "front": "In Lab 1, how many of the 200 pairs (N, 2N) have gap S_2N − S_N at least 1/2?", "back": "All 200 of 200 pairs.", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1 (finite range N = 1 to 200; Proposition 3.3 covers all N)"}
{"id": "M5-c033", "lesson": "L02", "front": "In Lab 1, from which index N does the geometric Cauchy test with ε = 1/1024 pass in the printed search?", "back": "N = 10. The test fails for N from 0 to 9.", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1 (search over m ≤ 60; Proposition 3.5 covers all m)"}
{"id": "M5-c034", "lesson": "L02", "front": "In Lab 1, how many pairs with 0 ≤ n < m ≤ 60 satisfy the identity of Lemma 3.4?", "back": "All 1830 pairs.", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1 (exact check on a finite range; Lemma 3.4 proves the identity)"}
{"id": "M5-c035", "lesson": "L03", "front": "What does it mean for f: D → R to be continuous at x0 in ε–δ form (Definition 1.1)?", "back": "For every ε > 0 there is δ > 0 such that each x in D with |x − x0| < δ satisfies |f(x) − f(x0)| < ε. The function is continuous on D when it is continuous at every point of D."}
{"id": "M5-c036", "lesson": "L03", "front": "What is uniform continuity of f on D (Section 3)?", "back": "For every ε > 0 there is one δ > 0 with |f(x) − f(y)| < ε whenever x and y are in D and |x − y| < δ. One δ serves all points at once."}
{"id": "M5-c037", "lesson": "L03", "front": "Lemma 1.3. Hypothesis: x is real with |x − 2| ≤ 1. What is the conclusion?", "back": "|x² − 4| ≤ 5|x − 2|.", "grade": "T", "basis": "Proof in Section 1 (Lemma 1.3)"}
{"id": "M5-c038", "lesson": "L03", "front": "Proposition 1.4. Hypothesis: D = R, f(x) = x², and x0 = 2. What is the conclusion?", "back": "f is continuous at 2, and δ = min{1, ε/5} works for each ε > 0.", "grade": "T", "basis": "Proof in Section 1 (Proposition 1.4)"}
{"id": "M5-c039", "lesson": "L03", "front": "Proposition 1.5. Hypotheses: f and g map D to R, x0 is in D, and both are continuous at x0. What is the conclusion?", "back": "f + g is continuous at x0.", "grade": "T", "basis": "Proof in Section 1 (Proposition 1.5)"}
{"id": "M5-c040", "lesson": "L03", "front": "Proposition 2.1 (sequential characterization). Hypotheses: D is a subset of R, f: D → R, and x0 is in D. What is the conclusion?", "back": "f is continuous at x0 if and only if f(x_n) → f(x0) for every sequence x_n in D with x_n → x0.", "grade": "T", "basis": "Proof in Section 2 (Proposition 2.1)"}
{"id": "M5-c041", "lesson": "L03", "front": "Proposition 2.2. Hypothesis: f(x) = x² on R and x_n → 2. What is the conclusion?", "back": "x_n² → 4.", "grade": "T", "basis": "Proof in Section 2 (Proposition 2.2)"}
{"id": "M5-c042", "lesson": "L03", "front": "Proposition 3.1. Hypothesis: f(x) = x² on R, with ε = 1. What is the conclusion?", "back": "For every δ > 0 there are x, y in R with |x − y| < δ and |f(x) − f(y)| ≥ 1. So x² is not uniformly continuous on R.", "grade": "T", "basis": "Proof in Section 3 (Proposition 3.1)"}
{"id": "M5-c043", "lesson": "L03", "front": "What is the least constant c with |x² − 4| ≤ c|x − 2| for every real x with |x − 2| ≤ 1?", "back": "c = 5. Lemma 1.3 gives the bound with 5, and at x = 3 the left side equals 5 while |x − 2| = 1, so no smaller c works (Problem R1).", "grade": "T", "basis": "Problem R1 (proof)"}
{"id": "M5-c044", "lesson": "L03", "front": "Does one δ serve every ε and every point?", "back": "No. δ depends on ε and on x0. The choice δ = min{1, ε/5} at the point 2 fails at the point 10 when ε = 1: with δ = 1/5, the point 10.1 lies within δ of 10, and |10.1² − 100| = 2.01 ≥ 1 (Misconception 1)."}
{"id": "M5-c045", "lesson": "L03", "front": "Is a function that is defined at x0 continuous there?", "back": "No. f(x) = x for x ≠ 1 and f(1) = 5 is defined at 1 but is not continuous there, because values near 1 are near 1, not near 5 (canon break 2)."}
{"id": "M5-c046", "lesson": "L03", "front": "Does one sequence converging to x0 with f(x_n) → f(x0) show continuity?", "back": "No. Proposition 2.1 needs every such sequence. For f = 0 at 0 and at each 1/k, and f = 1 elsewhere, the sequence x_n = 1/n gives f(x_n) = 0, but y_n = 2/(2n + 1) gives f(y_n) = 1 for every n (Misconception 3)."}
{"id": "M5-c047", "lesson": "L03", "front": "In Lab 1, how many grid points lie within δ = 1/50 of 2?", "back": "Seven points, namely k from −3 to 3.", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1 (exact grid 2 + k/200)"}
{"id": "M5-c048", "lesson": "L03", "front": "In Lab 1 with δ = 1/10, how many grid points have |x − 2| < 1/10 and |x² − 4| ≥ 1/10?", "back": "29 points.", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1 (exact grid; Problem E3 counts 15 and 14 on the two sides of 2)"}
{"id": "M5-c049", "lesson": "L03", "front": "Does the bound |x² − 4| ≤ 5|x − 2| hold at all 401 grid points in Lab 1?", "back": "Yes, at all 401 points. The grid is a finite check. Lemma 1.3 gives the bound for every x with |x − 2| ≤ 1.", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1 (exact grid); Lemma 1.3 is the proof"}
{"id": "M5-c050", "lesson": "L04", "front": "What are the three axioms of a metric on X (Definition 1.1)?", "back": "(M1) d(x, y) ≥ 0, with d(x, y) = 0 only when x = y; (M2) d(x, y) = d(y, x); (M3) d(x, z) ≤ d(x, y) + d(y, z) for all x, y, z."}
{"id": "M5-c051", "lesson": "L04", "front": "What are the open ball B(x, r) and an open set in a metric space (Section 2)?", "back": "B(x, r) = {y : d(x, y) < r}. A set U is open when every x in U has some r > 0 with B(x, r) inside U."}
{"id": "M5-c052", "lesson": "L04", "front": "When does a sequence (x_n) in a metric space converge to x (Definition 3.1)?", "back": "When d(x_n, x) → 0: for every ε > 0 there is N with d(x_n, x) < ε for all n ≥ N."}
{"id": "M5-c053", "lesson": "L04", "front": "What is a Cauchy sequence in a metric space (Definition 3.2)?", "back": "A sequence for which every ε > 0 has an N with d(x_m, x_n) < ε for all m, n ≥ N."}
{"id": "M5-c054", "lesson": "L04", "front": "When is a map f: X → Y between metric spaces continuous at x (Definition 4.1)?", "back": "For every ε > 0 there is δ > 0 with d_Y(f(x), f(z)) < ε whenever d_X(x, z) < δ."}
{"id": "M5-c055", "lesson": "L04", "front": "Proposition 1.4. Hypotheses: x, y, z are points of R², and d2 is the Euclidean distance |x − y|. What is the conclusion?", "back": "(R², d2) is a metric space. The triangle inequality follows from the Lagrange identity in two dimensions.", "grade": "T", "basis": "Proof in Section 1 (Proposition 1.4)"}
{"id": "M5-c056", "lesson": "L04", "front": "Proposition 1.7. Hypotheses: S is a nonempty set; f, g, h are bounded real functions on S; d∞(f, g) = sup over x in S of |f(x) − g(x)|; every nonempty subset of R bounded above has a supremum. What is the conclusion?", "back": "(ℓ∞(S), d∞) is a metric space, and every value d∞(f, g) is a real number.", "grade": "T", "basis": "Proof in Section 1 (Proposition 1.7)"}
{"id": "M5-c057", "lesson": "L04", "front": "Proposition 2.1. Hypotheses: (X, d) is a metric space; x is in X and r > 0. What is the conclusion?", "back": "The open ball B(x, r) is an open set.", "grade": "T", "basis": "Proof in Section 2 (Proposition 2.1)"}
{"id": "M5-c058", "lesson": "L04", "front": "Proposition 3.3. Hypotheses: (X, d) is a metric space; (x_n) converges to x in X. What is the conclusion?", "back": "(x_n) is a Cauchy sequence.", "grade": "T", "basis": "Proof in Section 3 (Proposition 3.3)"}
{"id": "M5-c059", "lesson": "L04", "front": "Proposition 3.4 (the plane is complete). Hypotheses: (x^(n)) is a Cauchy sequence in (R², d2); every Cauchy sequence of reals converges to a real number. What is the conclusion?", "back": "The sequence converges in R², so (R², d2) is complete.", "grade": "T", "basis": "Proof in Section 3 (Proposition 3.4)"}
{"id": "M5-c060", "lesson": "L04", "front": "Proposition 4.2 (continuity by sequences). Hypotheses: (X, d_X) and (Y, d_Y) are metric spaces, f: X → Y, and x is in X. What is the conclusion?", "back": "f is continuous at x if and only if d_Y(f(x_n), f(x)) → 0 for every sequence x_n in X with d_X(x_n, x) → 0.", "grade": "T", "basis": "Proof in Section 4 (Proposition 4.2)"}
{"id": "M5-c061", "lesson": "L04", "front": "Is (0, 1] with the Euclidean distance complete?", "back": "No. The sequence 1/n is Cauchy in (0, 1] and converges to 0 in R, but 0 is not in (0, 1] (Example 3.6).", "grade": "T", "basis": "Example 3.6 (proof on the page)"}
{"id": "M5-c062", "lesson": "L04", "front": "Is q(x, y) = (x − y)² a metric on R?", "back": "No. M1 and M2 hold, but M3 fails at the triple (0, 1, 2), since q(0, 2) = 4 exceeds q(0, 1) + q(1, 2) = 2.", "grade": "T", "basis": "Section 5 (canon break 1) and Lab 1 Part C; Empirical 2 lists the two failing triples"}
{"id": "M5-c063", "lesson": "L04", "front": "Is every symmetric function that vanishes only on the diagonal a distance?", "back": "No. The triangle inequality is an extra requirement. (x − y)² is symmetric and vanishes only at equal points, but it fails M3 (Misconception 1)."}
{"id": "M5-c064", "lesson": "L04", "front": "Does every Cauchy sequence converge?", "back": "Only in a complete space. In (0, 1] the Cauchy sequence 1/n has no limit in the space (Misconception 2)."}
{"id": "M5-c065", "lesson": "L04", "front": "Is a set open exactly when it is not closed?", "back": "No. The set (0, 1] in R is neither open nor closed. It is not open (Remark 2.2), and it is not closed, because every ball about 0 contains points of (0, 1] (Misconception 3)."}
{"id": "M5-c066", "lesson": "L04", "front": "In Lab 1 Part A, how many ordered triples does the Euclidean sample cover?", "back": "15625 triples, from the 25 grid points.", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1 Part A (finite sample; Proposition 1.4 covers all points)"}
{"id": "M5-c067", "lesson": "L04", "front": "In Lab 1 Part C, what do the printed values d(0, 2) and d(0, 1) + d(1, 2) show?", "back": "d(0, 2) = 4 and d(0, 1) + d(1, 2) = 2, so the triangle inequality fails at two triples.", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1 Part C (exact values on three points)"}
{"id": "M5-c068", "lesson": "L04", "front": "In Lab 1 Part B, how many vectors and triples does the sup-distance sample cover?", "back": "27 vectors and 19683 triples, with no failures.", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1 Part B (finite sample)"}
{"id": "M5-c190", "lesson": "L04", "front": "What is the sup distance d∞(f, g) between bounded real functions f and g on a nonempty set S (Example 1.6)?", "back": "d∞(f, g) = sup over x in S of |f(x) − g(x)|. It is a real number when f and g are bounded (Proposition 1.7), and on a finite S it is a maximum."}
{"id": "M5-c191", "lesson": "L01", "front": "What is a maximum of a set A of real numbers (Section 1)?", "back": "An element of A that is also an upper bound of A."}
{"id": "M5-c192", "lesson": "L04", "front": "Is d(x, y) = |x² − y²| on R a metric?", "back": "No. It satisfies M2 and M3, but d(1, −1) = 0 with 1 ≠ −1, so M1 fails and d is a pseudometric (Problem set, Proof 1).", "grade": "T", "basis": "Problem set, Proof 1 (L04)"}
{"id": "M5-c193", "lesson": "L04", "front": "When is a set of real numbers bounded, and when is a function bounded (Section 1)?", "back": "A set of real numbers is bounded when some real M ≥ 0 satisfies |v| ≤ M for every member v. A function is bounded when the set of its values is bounded."}
{"id": "M5-c201", "lesson": "L04", "front": "When is a metric space (X, d) complete (Definition 3.2)?", "back": "When every Cauchy sequence in X converges to a point of X."}
{"id": "M5-c213", "lesson": "L02", "front": "When is a sequence (s_n) of real numbers bounded (Section 2)?", "back": "When some real number C satisfies |s_n| ≤ C for every n."}
{"id": "M5-c214", "lesson": "L02", "front": "When is a set of real numbers bounded below (Section 2)?", "back": "When some real number m satisfies m ≤ b for every member b of the set. Such an m is a lower bound of the set."}
{"id": "M5-c215", "lesson": "L04", "front": "When is a subset K of a metric space X closed (Section 2)?", "back": "When its complement X ∖ K is open."}
{"id": "M5-c216", "lesson": "L01", "front": "Does Definition 1.1 (completeness) say anything about the set N of natural numbers?", "back": "No. N is nonempty but has no upper bound (Theorem 1.2), so the bounded-above hypothesis of Definition 1.1 fails for N.", "grade": "T", "basis": "Section 4 (canon break 3), from Theorem 1.2"}
{"id": "M5-c217", "lesson": "L02", "front": "In Lab 1, what is the smallest gap S_2N − S_N over N = 1 to 200, and at which N does it occur?", "back": "1/2, at N = 1.", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1 (finite range N = 1 to 200; Proposition 3.3 covers all N)"}
{"id": "M5-c218", "lesson": "L03", "front": "In Lab 1, do all grid points within δ = 1/50 of 2 satisfy |x² − 4| < 1/10?", "back": "Yes, all seven satisfy it.", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1 (exact grid 2 + k/200)"}
{"id": "M5-c219", "lesson": "L03", "front": "In Lab 1 with δ = 1/10, which is the first grid point with |x − 2| < 1/10 and |x² − 4| ≥ 1/10?", "back": "x = 381/200.", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1 (exact grid 2 + k/200)"}
{"id": "M5-c220", "lesson": "L04", "front": "In Lab 1 Part A, how many triangle-inequality failures are printed?", "back": "0.", "grade": "C", "basis": "Lab 1 Part A (finite sample; Proposition 1.4 covers all points)"}
